 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d27d639c-c470-59c3-a1f3-1df8d0bccc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313622} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce6d1d07-f029-55dd-b09c-cbbb98e0904e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bd3b7bd-f5a0-5a29-8c89-95a5d57c5496" :question/math-academy-id "q-339822" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $1$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}^{2}-2{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-2+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot (-2)+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee43064e-0f89-5585-a053-1d63767ced61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee43064e-0f89-5585-a053-1d63767ced61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313876} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d058b35-5591-5f8b-a767-e8b9b61eb060" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa6c947b-9991-5d44-84a4-afcc1fb08ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339842" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{arctan}\\,x\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =5(\\text{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})-\\text{arctan}(1)) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddf94f51-0ff1-50ae-bad8-f27b8dd41ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf94f51-0ff1-50ae-bad8-f27b8dd41ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313722} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d695fb2-0a1c-5813-8633-32337be30f6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45102bcc-b0f0-58bb-8720-e0d524eca593" :question/math-academy-id "q-339843" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3(arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2403bf92-33de-56e0-85ef-a581cb1a6312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312245} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2fc704-d0e9-53a6-8dcc-0e036a630979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312456} {:answer/id #uuid "6d581f87-d639-5aae-81d8-ac5d040ae6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d581f87-d639-5aae-81d8-ac5d040ae6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312836} :answer-field/id #uuid "5396f264-ea0d-5db9-b353-f86fbe694fe4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7206c63b-c464-5134-84a6-49aa5ac04832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312871} {:answer/id #uuid "7f92d116-7979-53c8-a931-6899507137ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313002} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed41681-02ae-5438-b215-763092f232ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed41681-02ae-5438-b215-763092f232ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a15b0a1-53a8-5905-b174-0fe6fa338599" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64ba9a3d-b554-5a79-ad69-4fec723c3b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-339865" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+2y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=3.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+6\\cdot (3.25)) \\\\  & =10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.25,\\,y(1)≈10.50.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1987c9ee-a7f8-50f9-a545-25e4dcbbcd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312159} {:answer/id #uuid "20a1a86a-cb58-5fdc-972a-4fb0b08350a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312222} {:answer/id #uuid "26870ffb-12d5-5e63-a931-434dc45c6e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1987c9ee-a7f8-50f9-a545-25e4dcbbcd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312159} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e726c7-c2a4-5b49-943a-bffcdfa1a1af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d3804a1-31a0-54e6-aedd-b16580fa2ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312835} {:answer/id #uuid "8750b333-5234-51fb-b4ac-50415c2125b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313056} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ef3dbc-8ff6-5b7b-9bd0-5d1c554139d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d3804a1-31a0-54e6-aedd-b16580fa2ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312835} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ea1a90-a4f9-5314-ac16-6781e319b64c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fd2de3b-c285-5953-b35a-1631bb0e5b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339867" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =1.30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+3\\cdot (1.30)) \\\\  & =1.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.08.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbc7a91-b04a-5d0a-ab60-07d245bddb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312335} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9869da-d045-5377-9e44-55ed8d6afff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313314} {:answer/id #uuid "b583f664-6d43-506e-85b8-d4aaf90c2bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b583f664-6d43-506e-85b8-d4aaf90c2bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313387} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2b02ba-b283-5602-8a97-9aad598c3a7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba3893c-fe5c-52da-a6bd-64be557ff52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-124.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312180} {:answer/id #uuid "440ea820-9647-5812-b3ae-9bbf40d7797b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-111.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312523} {:answer/id #uuid "72d1d36c-2495-5036-821e-6d50799d57d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-122.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ba3893c-fe5c-52da-a6bd-64be557ff52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-124.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312180} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ec2869-77fd-5286-a4ab-9e9e7155bcae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "766f6cd8-a666-5ac2-92f9-bc5ddd8560c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-339868" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+5y,\\,y(1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+5y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+9\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 13) \\\\  & =-124.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈13.00,\\,y(2)≈-124.00.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73ffbef5-670e-5b43-8085-11ebb12b962e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312897} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab1000-6847-56ca-87cc-11d6d8e28a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313447} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e27a90-0a77-53f1-81fc-51180d0cd9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8e27a90-0a77-53f1-81fc-51180d0cd9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313829} :answer-field/id #uuid "378a03ef-145b-5a5d-8fa5-e903d464e8c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826de63c-65ce-5b8e-bdf5-ec0609ee7b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313026} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ab2b3f-caa2-5bdb-abd4-bc797d65298e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313674} {:answer/id #uuid "e85c5b8d-635c-5190-b6b9-f372df08fde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826de63c-65ce-5b8e-bdf5-ec0609ee7b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313026} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05d9bd4-f9cd-5c8e-bec5-f50b0b58de10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56bd69c9-5c91-506a-8565-7dbf3ccfd848" :question/math-academy-id "q-339869" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3x+y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-3x+y)+(-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{1}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{4})$ and $y(\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4},\\,y=3.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3x-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+9\\cdot (3.75)) \\\\  & =4.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈3.75,\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.50.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083e729d-02a0-5b64-b9d1-0bd9cf3add1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312034} {:answer/id #uuid "27234f87-c873-50fa-b102-0f7d110fb6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312270} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff2de4-770e-5ef6-9e26-792c8390e242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "083e729d-02a0-5b64-b9d1-0bd9cf3add1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312034} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b87b810-bcf8-5de6-a510-d61fcbc3a6ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169a7093-c262-533a-b6b3-a2b652f192f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312130} {:answer/id #uuid "262991c7-0acb-51ed-9a18-bb6d484e0851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312265} {:answer/id #uuid "559ff809-92f2-5c10-b5df-212349a37415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262991c7-0acb-51ed-9a18-bb6d484e0851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312265} :answer-field/id #uuid "be070c66-cc79-53f5-bd76-183037ee7057" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7de34b1b-1b77-5668-9411-13a471ca2f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339871" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=25x+5y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{2}{5})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((25x+5y)+(25{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y(\\frac{2}{5}).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{5},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{5})≈1.00,\\,y(\\frac{2}{5})≈6.00.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09797b8b-4847-5003-adf4-243e44b2236b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312044} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3b1f1f-f3cc-5f01-b27f-6db2ff81d4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312728} {:answer/id #uuid "762b3b2e-11c3-5a87-82ea-9f879d5dfed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312919} {:answer/id #uuid "bb522802-18f4-5695-b0b2-37dd3b41cc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09797b8b-4847-5003-adf4-243e44b2236b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312044} :answer-field/id #uuid "355e30b4-dcc5-5384-8098-953b613193c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50469212-57da-54a4-898c-5e445181e007" :question/math-academy-id "q-339872" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2{x}^{3}+3),\\,y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(0),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(0)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(2{x}^{3}+3)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+3)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+3) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2{x}^{3}+7)}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+7)}{1-2\\cdot {0}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & ≈2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈2.25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cf00962-b6b4-5998-9975-1454f9f132c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf00962-b6b4-5998-9975-1454f9f132c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312597} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd890a5-ae47-5a39-8de8-b66ed079ea1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e13b26-bfd5-5474-9a46-4ad33e13d1cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340137" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55bf33bc-86e0-5cb8-9d56-e6d5bfb12d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55bf33bc-86e0-5cb8-9d56-e6d5bfb12d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312664} :answer-field/id #uuid "592e9e2a-abdb-5b4b-b338-481f7bd7b8cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd956af4-b2b4-5ae8-a37f-585154ac194f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340471" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)+\\frac{1}{10}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4d8bc8a-5390-5efc-9e59-40435b355e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4d8bc8a-5390-5efc-9e59-40435b355e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313654} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71dfe58-5be5-56c6-a2e6-847bd2c5dd2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9bb2c48-7d90-5eb1-aae2-2d07b2d4d022" :question/math-academy-id "q-340472" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e6828d-7887-5550-bbca-792a725051cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e6828d-7887-5550-bbca-792a725051cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313239} :answer-field/id #uuid "82e86e27-51c1-5064-9e7c-9cbb5e3878b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f1aff49-4ed8-5efc-a461-ed82ccac840e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340474" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{20}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f042c507-b55b-5c8c-8978-63c9054e0c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f042c507-b55b-5c8c-8978-63c9054e0c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313900} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e22fb33-417f-5805-ab4b-38d45822ffdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "391451cc-b516-5347-88a9-b2eff039d5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-340487" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x+3x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{8}(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d074817c-9881-518c-b1ea-75ba1846c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d074817c-9881-518c-b1ea-75ba1846c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313605} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1a4a9f-47b6-58b2-8ec5-aab72b4b9f7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11434a0f-ad34-5cb2-96ed-81af7783f37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340492" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{12}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc99873b-3451-5f41-ba37-844fe33217e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc99873b-3451-5f41-ba37-844fe33217e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313989} :answer-field/id #uuid "018d0f8e-d1f2-519a-966e-53e0db26ecbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2e94a3f-da6f-5c4b-aae5-24125dac827b" :question/math-academy-id "q-340505" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-8x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+8x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{22}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(1-0)-\\frac{1}{22}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0565c9a1-3267-5c8e-9342-8d4792792693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312010} {:answer/id #uuid "30337cd4-b663-516c-812c-f06d183d5cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312364} {:answer/id #uuid "94928d5f-97ee-5b8d-89ac-d044f0019c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313167} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c85d6-087e-5c2a-b134-ecf511b1d930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313344} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d77545-249a-5fbd-a02a-be4c5c92046d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30337cd4-b663-516c-812c-f06d183d5cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312364} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d647f9e-721a-5afb-a7d1-b8fcf37b997b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cd19394-53ef-5fb8-bd17-0eb0faf26bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340560" :question/problem "The rate at which the price of a particular product changes $P(t),$ after $t$ years, is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nIf the price today is $$120,$ how many years will it take for the price to increase to $$750?$\nRound your answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "We model the price growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.042t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the price today is $$120,$ so we have ${P}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the price $P$ of the product after $t$ years is\n\n$$\nP(t)=120\\,{e}^{0.042t}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find how many years it takes for the price to grow to $$750,$ we substitute $P(t)=750$ into the equation above and solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}750 & =120\\,{e}^{0.042t} \\\\ \\frac{750}{120} & ={e}^{0.042t} \\\\ {e}^{0.042t} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.042t}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ 0.042t & =ln\\,(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{0.042}ln\\,(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ t & ≈43.633\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $44$ years for the price to increase to $$750.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72bf0eb-ad62-5881-9084-66b56ea503b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72bf0eb-ad62-5881-9084-66b56ea503b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313672} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5eea6c3-db40-505b-9e0d-14232418925e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8691b6d-0ecf-5d21-b2f3-79779cf5dbfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-340599" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{ln⁡(x)}{cot⁡(x)}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $cot⁡(x)\\,+{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\cdot ${{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $x{cot}^{2}⁡(x)$ |" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\frac{ln⁡(x)}{cot⁡(x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{cot⁡(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-ln⁡(x)(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x))}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{cot⁡(x)+xln⁡(x){csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{x})}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{cot⁡(x)+{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot xln⁡(x)}{x{cot}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a88a36db-5e45-548d-8bfc-2a65d42b2b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{csc}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a88a36db-5e45-548d-8bfc-2a65d42b2b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{csc}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313297} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb36ddb-0276-5ac3-89fe-420e530a39fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e33e2c96-5cc5-507d-8909-c27b983d8b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-340603" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{x}{cot⁡(x)}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $cot⁡(x)\\,+${{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | ${cot}^{2}⁡(x)$ |" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{cot⁡(x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(cot⁡(x))-x(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x))}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{cot⁡(x)+x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faba1883-af56-500b-a141-28392efb394f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faba1883-af56-500b-a141-28392efb394f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313976} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7953c8-2aeb-585d-85e3-e06ca80b78e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19b497f4-a444-5fb7-80fe-a52cd36cf318" :question/math-academy-id "q-340660" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ if $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{sec⁡x}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4x}{sec⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)\\cdot sec⁡x-4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sec⁡x)}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4sec⁡x-4x\\cdot sec⁡xtan⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4sec⁡x(1-xtan⁡x)}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-xtan⁡x)}{sec⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=0$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{4(1-0\\cdot tan⁡0)}{sec⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-0)}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73087ad3-084c-52a2-997b-89a204724d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312884} {:answer/id #uuid "8afb6427-9388-5852-9036-c08f4624949b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313080} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbda9a0-cf5b-500c-a119-83b819ce706d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313476} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8ef8e8-fa17-5d92-bde5-6dd1b36f5d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313563} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b93f7b-da80-573c-9869-a1204d3768ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb8ef8e8-fa17-5d92-bde5-6dd1b36f5d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313563} :answer-field/id #uuid "24d600cd-0e54-560b-afd6-952cc5040549" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-